1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Napoleon’s side of the ledger was thin. The French interloper had few out- right local allies. The most important local friend was one of Zahir al-Umar’s sons, Abbas ibn Zahir al-Umar—together with 300 cavalrymen, 
2 CONC Abbas had shown up at Napoleon’s Hill just at the start of the French siege on Acre, and offered his services. Abbas, who had quarreled with al-Jazzar and the Ottomans, and had thus been hiding in Druze villages in Mount Lebanon, 
2 CONC was appointed “Sheikh of Tiberias” by Napoleon. Enjoying a measure of local support, Abbas was probably Napoleon’s best card in a bid to win Muslim support in Palestine.
2 CONT Ill-advisedly, the French allowed a less popular Muslim figure, Mustafa Bashir (another former protégé of al-Jazzar who had fallen out of favor with his suspicious mentor), to wield control in Safed. Local residents had been hopin
2 CONC g that Abbas’s rule would be extended.
2 CONT
2 CONT The History of Galilee, 1538–1949 _ Mysticism, -- Matthew M_ Silver -- Rowman & Littlefield Publishing, Lanham, Maryland, 2022 -- Lexington 
